I took a day off to code on my own. I realized I like to code and missed it, and that realization comes with unintended consequences. What is different about me coding? I code in kant and in doing so that process evolves in a good way. I write kant the way I want it to work, which may not be how it works, and hitting edges that do not work, so coding kant becomes an immediate feedback process with ramifications.
For example, hitting what might be a hard edge sends me into the code affected to have a look. Increasingly, that is becoming unsavory. Code that I was very familiar with is turning less familiar in an ugly way as simple rule actions get gated to take on responsibilities not originally intended. Generating and jitting have a downside wrt mess inflicted on innocent code w/gating and confusing the flow of code, not to mention it adds more C++ when the end objective is reduce or eliminate C++ in favor of move to kant.
And kant is better than it was. The way it works is becoming more like how I want it to work. Less work arounds required. It still needs buttressing with C++ commands, but those commands tend trivially short. And kant code keeps getting more economical wrt the amount of code you need to write to get things done. Hammer and tongs will continue to be needed to continue that becoming better process.
